Align the LABEL stack to the pointer, not to the cell.

The operand stack is uint32-granular, and the label stack is built by casting its
end:

    frame_csp = frame->csp_bottom = (WASMBranchBlock *)frame->sp_boundary;

A WASMBranchBlock is made of pointers. Where a pointer is wider than a cell -- 16
bytes on Capstone, needing 16-byte alignment -- that cast lands under-aligned and
the first store into the label stack faults. For a function with no parameters, no
locals and two stack cells the misalignment is exactly 8, which is what three runs
measured.

Pad the operand stack so the cast is aligned. The padding cells flow into
all_cell_num, which is what sizes the frame and what the stack-headroom check uses,
so nothing is undersized; the loader caps every input at UINT16_MAX and the pad is
at most three cells. Exactly one site performs the cast, so this is one fix.

WAMR_LABEL_STACK_PAD is a knob rather than a constant so the four-arm experiment
that attributes the fault to this pad can be built from ONE tree with one variable
changing. It defaults to on.

Guarded by __SIZEOF_POINTER__ > 8, so it is a no-op everywhere upstream runs.
diff --git a/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp_classic.c b/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp_classic.c
index 5d23142..ede538f 100644
--- a/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp_classic.c
+++ b/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp_classic.c
@@ -6787,6 +6787,23 @@ wasm_interp_call_func_bytecode(WASMModuleInstance *module,
             max_stack_cell_num += eh_size;
 #endif
 
+#ifndef WAMR_LABEL_STACK_PAD
+#define WAMR_LABEL_STACK_PAD 1
+#endif
+#if defined(__SIZEOF_POINTER__) && __SIZEOF_POINTER__ > 8 && WAMR_LABEL_STACK_PAD
+            /* csp_bottom is (WASMBranchBlock *)frame->sp_boundary, and a branch
+               block is made of pointers. sp_boundary is uint32-granular, so where
+               a pointer is wider than a cell the label stack starts under-aligned
+               and the first store into it faults. Pad the operand stack so the
+               cast lands aligned; the padding cells go into all_cell_num below,
+               so the frame still fits what is written into it. */
+            {
+                uint32 cells = (uint32)(sizeof(void *) / 4);
+                uint32 upto = cur_func->param_cell_num
+                              + cur_func->local_cell_num + max_stack_cell_num;
+                max_stack_cell_num += (cells - upto % cells) % cells;
+            }
+#endif
             cell_num_of_local_stack = cur_func->param_cell_num
                                       + cur_func->local_cell_num
                                       + max_stack_cell_num;
